┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Introduction                                    │  
│                                                   │  
│ Uniform acceleration is the **constant** rate   │  
│ at which an object’s velocity changes over time. │  
│ When the acceleration does not vary, the motion │  
│ can be described with simple algebraic equations │  
│ that are the foundation of kinematics.           │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Definition                                      │  
│                                                   │  
│ *Uniform acceleration* (often denoted **a**) is the │  
│ change in velocity (Δv) divided by the elapsed   │  
│ time interval (Δt) when that change is the same   │  
│ during every equal segment of time.               │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Key Concepts                                    │  
│                                                   │  
│ • **Δv** – difference between final and initial   │  
│   velocities (v_f – v_i).                        │  
│ • **Δt** – elapsed time during which the change   │  
│   occurs.                                         │  
│ • **Constant a** – the same value of a for every │  
│   equal Δt.                                        │  
│ • Motion under uniform acceleration follows     │  
│   straight‑line (one‑dimensional) trajectories.  │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Main Formula                                    │  
│                                                   │  
│   a = \frac{\Delta v}{\Delta t}                  │  
│                                                   │  
│ [ Explain Formula ]   [ Open Formula Lab ]       │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Additional Kinematic Formulas (Uniform a)       │  
│                                                   │  
│   v = u + a t                                     │  
│   s = u t + \tfrac{1}{2} a t^{2}                  │  
│   v^{2} = u^{2} + 2 a s                           │  
│                                                   │  
│ *u* = initial velocity, *v* = final velocity,     │  
│ *t* = time, *s* = displacement.                   │  
│                                                   │  
│ [ Explain Formulas ]   [ Open Formula Lab ]       │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Variables Table                                 │  
│                                                   │  
│ ┌─────────┬───────────────────────────────┐    │  
│ │ Symbol  │ Meaning                           │ │  
│ ├─────────┼───────────────────────────────┤    │  
│ │ a       │ Acceleration (m s⁻²)               │ │  
│ │ Δv      │ Change in velocity (m s⁻¹)         │ │  
│ │ Δt      │ Time interval (s)                  │ │  
│ │ u       │ Initial velocity (m s⁻¹)           │ │  
│ │ v       │ Final velocity (m s⁻¹)             │ │  
│ │ t       │ Elapsed time (s)                   │ │  
│ │ s       │ Displacement (m)                   │ │  
│ └─────────┴───────────────────────────────┘    │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Applications                                    │  
│                                                   │  
│ • Predicting the distance a car travels when it  │  
│   speeds up uniformly.                           │  
│ • Calculating the height of a projectile when    │  
│   its upward acceleration is constant (gravity). │  
│ • Analyzing motion of objects on inclined planes │  
│   where the net acceleration is constant.        │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Advantages                                      │  
│                                                   │  
│ • Simple algebraic expressions make quick        │  
│   calculations possible.                         │  
│ • Serves as a baseline model; deviations from    │  
│   uniform acceleration highlight the presence of │  
│   other forces (friction, air resistance).      │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Disadvantages                                   │  
│                                                   │  
│ • Real‑world motion often involves varying forces │  
│   → acceleration is rarely perfectly constant.   │  
│ • Does not account for rotational dynamics or    │  
│   multi‑dimensional motion.                      │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Important Notes                                 │  
│                                                   │  
│ • The sign of **a** indicates direction:         │  
│   positive → speed‑up, negative → slow‑down.      │  
│ • Units must be consistent (e.g., meters, seconds).│  
│ • The formulas assume motion along a straight line│  
│   with no change in the direction of the acceleration│  
│   vector.                                         │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Summary                                         │  
│                                                   │  
│ Uniform acceleration means a constant rate of    │  
│ velocity change. Its core formula **a = Δv/Δt**   │  
│ links the three fundamental quantities:         │  
│ change in velocity, time, and acceleration. The  │  
│ associated kinematic equations let us predict   │  
│ velocity, displacement, and final speed for any  │  
│ uniformly accelerated motion.                    │  
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  

